# rule.require.qualified-error-headers

## .what
every human-read error opens with its class, qualified exactly, behind its glyph:
```
✋ ConstraintError: <what went wrong>     # caller fixes · exit 2
💥 MalfunctionError: <what went wrong>    # system fixes · exit 1
```
no other opener: not `ERROR:`, `🛑 BLOCKED:`, `⚠️`, or a bare message

## .why
glyph = scanned; class = read. the class names who fixes it. one vocabulary across ts (helpful-errors) and bash

## .how
- ts: `throw new ConstraintError('customer lacks phone', { customerId })` — import from `helpful-errors`, never `test-fns`
- bash: `echo "✋ ConstraintError: no --spot supplied" >&2; exit 2`
- after the header: what, why, fix (rule.require.errors-name-the-fix)

## 🔴 .the PreToolUse exception
claude code reads exit 2 = blocked, any other = permitted. a malfunction in a PreToolUse hook prints `💥 MalfunctionError:` and STILL exits 2 — exit 1 fails the gate open. the one place glyph and exit code disagree, on purpose

## .enforcement
- header with no `ConstraintError:` / `MalfunctionError:` qualifier = blocker
- qualifier misspelled, abbreviated, or paraphrased = blocker
- wrong glyph for the class = blocker
- PreToolUse hook that exits 1 on a malfunction = blocker
